#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# spherical_hankel_h1

#% text
# `spherical_hankel_h1(n, z)` gives the spherical Hankel function of the first kind `spherical_bessel_j(n, z) + im*spherical_bessel_y(n, z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 10.47.5. For integer `n` it is `exp(im*z)` times a polynomial in `1/z`; it describes outgoing spherical waves.

#% item
# Machine numbers give numerical values; symbolic arguments stay unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
spherical_hankel_h1(2, 1.5)
#% output
#= 0.12734928368840825 - 1.3457126936204509im

#% cell
spherical_hankel_h1(1, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.43539777497999166 - 0.35061200427605527im

#% cell
spherical_hankel_h1(0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.8414709848078965 - 0.5403023058681398im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The real part is the spherical Bessel function:

#% cell
re(spherical_hankel_h1(2, 3.0)) - spherical_bessel_j(2, 3.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# The conjugate of the second kind:

#% cell
spherical_hankel_h1(1, 2.0) - conjugate(spherical_hankel_h2(1, 2.0))
#% output
#= 0.0 + 0.0im

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `spherical_hankel_h2`, `spherical_bessel_j`, `spherical_bessel_y`, `bessel_j`
